\subsection{Architecture-Level Ablation}
\label{sec:arch_results}

We test whether critic capacity and Double Q pessimism \citep{fujimoto2018td3} address CIVO (\S\ref{sec:dual_critic_method}; Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}). Across all dual-critic configurations ($n{=}5$, three environments), the two critics converge to near-identical value functions ($\rho > 0.999$), with per-sample pessimism at 3--4\% of the independent-critic bound. Both critics train on shared RSSM features with no real-data anchor, eliminating the representational diversity underlying Double Q's effectiveness in model-free RL.

Despite pessimism failure, the min operation enables CIVO \emph{recovery}: narrow dual-critic (exp\_035) experiences CIVO ($\nve = 2.75\times$) but recovers fully (eval 697.8), outperforming wide single-critic (exp\_033, eval 407.9) with $2\times$ fewer parameters. CIVO \emph{prevention} requires min $+$ capacity synergy: only wide dual-critic (exp\_032, 3 seeds) maintains $\nve \leq 1.4\times$ throughout. Cross-environment transfer is limited: on Cheetah, wide dual-critic reduces NVE from $8.27\times$ to $1.75\times$ but does not prevent onset (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}). Prevention does not guarantee stability: 1/3 Walker seeds avoids CIVO ($\nve \leq 0.3\times$) yet collapses via non-CIVO policy destabilization (eval $354 \to 120.9$, entropy increase), yielding 2/3 performance stability despite 3/3 CIVO prevention.

