\paragraph{CIVO mechanism and gradient analysis.}
The RSSM develops a $2.78\times$ reward prediction gap under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), creating a model-induced feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional}. Gradient decomposition (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_decomp}) shows the reward head contributes 38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$ yet reward-only SGS fails (NVE $10.42\times$), while the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal. All SGS variants exhibit compensatory amplification; full-pathway SGS (0/3) establishes forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability floor (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{Gradient magnitude $\neq$ causal importance.}
The disconnect between gradient share and causal influence generalizes to multi-head architectures sharing learned representations: gradient-based attribution would direct intervention to the reward head (largest share, 38--49\%), but ablation reveals the observation decoder (16--22\%) as the critical pathway. For multi-head world models, causal ablation is necessary; gradient norms alone are misleading diagnostics. Relatedly, the trained embedding creates at least two mechanistically independent failure pathways (\S\ref{sec:f6}), confirming that temporal conditioning introduces structural vulnerability beyond any single mechanism.

\paragraph{Why not standard overestimation countermeasures?}
Double Q-learning \citep{vanhasselt2016double}, TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3}, and ensemble critics \citep{chen2021redq} target \emph{estimation error} in the critic's training dynamics. CIVO is upstream: the RSSM's $2.78\times$ fidelity gap biases the critic's \emph{inputs}. Our dual-critic ablation (\S\ref{sec:arch_results}) confirms this empirically: two critics trained on shared RSSM features converge to $\rho > 0.999$ correlation, so the min operation cannot provide meaningful pessimism. The min operation nonetheless enables CIVO recovery through a different mechanism (likely implicit value regularization during early training), while prevention requires min $+$ increased capacity in combination. MBPO \citep{janner2019mbpo} mixes real data, but DreamerV3 trains entirely within imagination. MOReL \citep{kidambi2020morel} and COMBO \citep{yu2021combo} penalize uncertainty, but the RSSM is \emph{confidently wrong} at $\dt > 1$.

\paragraph{MPC vs.\ imagination-based actor-critic.}
TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} succeeds with $\dt$-conditioning in TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} because MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic. In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination: the RSSM's fidelity gap at $\dt > 1$ produces biased value estimates that the actor exploits, closing a positive feedback loop with no MPC counterpart (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
The intervention hierarchy (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}) yields actionable recommendations: (1)~monitor for CIVO using the NVE diagnostic ($\geq 1.5\times$, AUROC${=}0.971$, $n{=}29$; Appendix~\ref{sec:roc_analysis}); (2)~wide dual-critic architecture achieves the highest prevention rate (3/4) but fails on Cheetah and 1/3 prevented seeds still collapse via non-CIVO mechanisms; (3)~TCN ($\sigma{=}0.2$) achieves complete suppression in 2 of 4 Walker seeds but fails cross-environment; (4)~avoid gradient-level interventions (compensatory amplification) and output-level value capping (anchor pollution).

